Dispatches from O'Reilly: The right amount of spec for agentic ...
  URL: https://stackoverflow.blog/2026/08/21/dispatches-from-o-reilly-the-right-amount-of-spec-for-agentic-development/
  I keep seeing the same idea in conversations about agents: Detailed specs are old-world overhead now.

That’s why specification suddenly feels important again.

They talk as if the sequence is simple: write the spec, then let the agent implement it.

That prompt usually produces polished fog. A better prompt is much more specific:

At that point, the spec is no longer just guidance but more like a contract.

That contract needs more than a paragraph of intent. It needs schemas, invariants, allowed ambiguity, validation rules, and explicit failure behavior.

Otherwise, you end up with two specs.

In that case, I would rather specify boundaries than outcomes: what must be true, what must not happen, what evidence is required, and which decisions still need a human.

Every boundary between agents needs a contract.

When agents can make very large changes cheaply, the temptation is to accept very large diffs cheaply too.

Agents can make implementation dramatically cheaper, but once code gets cheap, specification and verification become the place where projects succeed or fail.

They’ll be the teams that know when three bullets are enough, when they need a real contract, and when the contract has to become executable.

